## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates testing approaches for real-time ML moderation systems with canary deployments, privacy requirements, and drift detection capabilities—extending beyond basic functional testing.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end latency and throughput optimization
- Data privacy and PII masking protocols
- Canary deployment strategies with automated rollback
- Model drift detection and monitoring systems
- Contract testing between microservices and components

## Code Example
```javascript
// Latency contract validation (pseudo-code)
async function validateLatencyContract(moderationRequest) {
  const startTime = Date.now();
  const result = await moderationService.process(moderationRequest);
  const latency = Date.now() - startTime;

  if (latency > 200) {
    throw new Error(`SLA breach: ${latency}ms > 200ms`);
  }

  return { result, latency };
}

// Drift detection monitoring
function detectModelDrift(currentMetrics, baselineMetrics) {
  const precisionDrift = Math.abs(currentMetrics.precision - baselineMetrics.precision);
  const recallDrift = Math.abs(currentMetrics.recall - baselineMetrics.recall);

  return precisionDrift > 0.05 || recallDrift > 0.05;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Explores testing considerations for feature flags and deployment safety, a practical beginner-level scenario relevant to modern cloud services.

## Key Concepts
- Flag-driven code paths and environment-based config
- Unit vs integration vs contract testing
- Canary/staging validation and rollback safety
- Deterministic data and payload coverage

## Code Example
```javascript
// Jest skeleton
describe('POST /process with flag', () => {
  test('uses A when USE_NEW_ALGO=false', async () => {
    process.env.USE_NEW_ALGO = 'false';
    const res = await request(app).post('/process').send({ input: 1 });
    expect(res.status).toBe(200);
    expect(res.body.algorithm).toBe('A');
  });
  test('uses B when USE_NEW_ALGO=true', async () => {
    process.env.USE_NEW_ALGO = 'true';
    const res = await request(app).post('/process').send({ input: 1 });
    expect(res.body.algorithm).toBe('B');
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt tests if the flag is sourced from a remote config service instead of env vars?
- What metrics would you collect to ensure the canary deployment remains within acceptable latency targets?

## Why This Is Asked

Real-world payments pipelines rely on idempotent, replay-safe processing across multiple services. This question probes practical test design for exactly-once-like semantics in a streaming reconciliation workflow.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency and deduplication in event-driven flows
- Exactly-once-like semantics via transactional writes
- End-to-end replay safety and offset management
